WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.075
Authorization is where many otherwise
well-built Flask applications fail.

2
00:00:08.155 --> 00:00:10.719
A route may require login,

3
00:00:10.799 --> 00:00:18.491
but that does not prove the caller can use
the specific object named in the URL or

4
00:00:18.571 --> 00:00:19.969
request body.

5
00:00:20.049 --> 00:00:26.692
Secure authorization checks the caller,
the action, the resource,

6
00:00:26.772 --> 00:00:29.336
and the relevant context.

7
00:00:29.416 --> 00:00:37.225
Context may include tenant membership,
account ownership, role, permission,

8
00:00:37.305 --> 00:00:41.733
workflow state, environment, sensitivity,

9
00:00:41.813 --> 00:00:49.272
and whether the action is normal user
behavior or administrative behavior.

10
00:00:49.352 --> 00:00:56.927
Role-based access can be useful, but roles
are usually only part of the model.

11
00:00:57.007 --> 00:01:04.582
A support role may view some customer
information but not export all records.

12
00:01:04.662 --> 00:01:10.839
A team admin may manage users within one
tenant but not another.

13
00:01:10.919 --> 00:01:16.047
A project member may read an object but
not delete it.

14
00:01:16.127 --> 00:01:23.119
Object-level authorization ties the
decision to the specific record,

15
00:01:23.199 --> 00:01:28.444
file, workspace, tenant, or resource being
touched.

16
00:01:28.524 --> 00:01:36.798
Without it, an application can have good
login security and still expose data across

17
00:01:36.878 --> 00:01:38.160
boundaries.

18
00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:45.349
Flask developers often use decorators,
blueprints, helper functions,

19
00:01:45.429 --> 00:01:51.256
and service-layer guards to make
authorization reusable.

20
00:01:51.336 --> 00:01:59.028
Those patterns are helpful when they make
enforcement consistent and close to

21
00:01:59.108 --> 00:02:01.089
sensitive actions.

22
00:02:01.169 --> 00:02:06.763
They are risky when they create a false
sense of coverage.

23
00:02:06.843 --> 00:02:11.971
Hiding a button, removing a link,
obscuring a route,

24
00:02:12.051 --> 00:02:17.529
or relying on client-side checks is not
access control.

25
00:02:17.609 --> 00:02:25.650
The server must enforce authorization even
if a request is manually constructed.

26
00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:29.926
Authorization should be tested directly.

27
00:02:30.006 --> 00:02:35.600
A good test suite includes normal access,
denied access,

28
00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:42.672
cross-tenant attempts, object ownership
checks, privilege changes,

29
00:02:42.752 --> 00:02:49.861
administrative boundaries, and negative
cases for sensitive actions.

30
00:02:49.941 --> 00:02:54.137
These tests are not offensive procedures;

31
00:02:54.217 --> 00:03:01.442
they are defensive assurance that the
application follows its own rules.

32
00:03:01.522 --> 00:03:06.883
When teams make authorization explicit and
testable,

33
00:03:06.963 --> 00:03:13.140
Flask routes become easier to reason about
and safer to extend.
